<Configuration>
  <!--
    Test the APP in a clean Windows, without the 6 GB download.

    Companion to test-install.wsb, which tests the installer. That one has to
    download the payload, and sandbox networking runs about twenty times
    slower than the host, so a full run there costs most of a day.

    Once the installer has been seen to START downloading, the question it
    exists to answer is settled: the Hugging Face url compiled into the exe is
    correct. Everything after that is bytes, and the payload is already
    verified server-side by size and SHA512.

    What is left to check is the app itself, and that does not need the
    installer at all. This maps the already-unpacked build straight in.

    Launch it (double-click often does nothing, the .wsb handler is not always
    registered):

        & "$env:WINDIR\System32\WindowsSandbox.exe" scripts\test-app.wsb

    Then, inside: open the ClipsStudio folder on the desktop and run
    "Clips Kitty.exe".

    Still a clean Windows: no Python, no Ollama, no FFmpeg, none of your files.
    So it answers the real question, whether the app runs somewhere none of its
    dependencies were ever installed.

    CUDA still does not work in a sandbox, so expect it to report no GPU.
  -->
  <MappedFolders>
    <MappedFolder>
      <!-- Read-only: this is the build that was shipped, and a test must not
           be able to modify it. The app writes to %LOCALAPPDATA% rather than
           beside its executable, so read-only is enough for it to run. -->
      <HostFolder>C:\Users\colin\YouTube-Clips-Automation\release\win-unpacked</HostFolder>
      <SandboxFolder>C:\Users\WDAGUtilityAccount\Desktop\ClipsStudio</SandboxFolder>
      <ReadOnly>true</ReadOnly>
    </MappedFolder>
  </MappedFolders>

  <!-- Needed: the first-run wizard pulls the language model. -->
  <Networking>Enable</Networking>

  <vGPU>Enable</vGPU>

  <MemoryInMB>8192</MemoryInMB>
</Configuration>
